00:00:16.930 --> 00:00:18.490
team.

00:00:18.490 --> 00:00:22.930
So we're here to talk about
container native load balancing

00:00:22.930 --> 00:00:24.910
on Kubernetes engine.

00:00:24.910 --> 00:00:27.100
We have three items
on the agenda.

00:00:27.100 --> 00:00:30.490
First, how Google
Cloud treats containers

00:00:30.490 --> 00:00:33.490
as first-class loader
balancer endpoints.

00:00:33.490 --> 00:00:37.420
And second, how Kubernetes
engine natively supports

00:00:37.420 --> 00:00:40.480
advanced level seven
functionalities.

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:42.700
And finally, we have
a surprise session

00:00:42.700 --> 00:00:44.170
with something coming soon.

00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:51.640
In 2015, Kubernetes
1.0 was launched.

00:00:51.640 --> 00:00:53.890
Google brings its
container experience

00:00:53.890 --> 00:00:56.410
of over a decade
to the industry.

00:00:56.410 --> 00:00:58.870
As an open source
project, Kubernetes

00:00:58.870 --> 00:01:00.750
focused on portability.

00:01:00.750 --> 00:01:04.750
It uses the lowest common
denominator technology.

00:01:04.750 --> 00:01:08.050
That includes load balancing.

00:01:08.050 --> 00:01:11.260
In 2015, most load
balancers only

00:01:11.260 --> 00:01:15.220
work with virtual
machines and bare metals.

00:01:15.220 --> 00:01:19.030
They do not support
containers natively.

00:01:19.030 --> 00:01:21.550
Load balancing
solutions for Kubernetes

00:01:21.550 --> 00:01:24.430
usually involve
some kind of proxy

00:01:24.430 --> 00:01:29.020
to pass traffic from the load
balancer to the container.

00:01:29.020 --> 00:01:32.410
In summary, when
Kubernetes was born,

00:01:32.410 --> 00:01:35.470
load balancing solutions
was not optimized.

00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:43.020
Imagine you are a customer
with multi-tier application.

00:01:43.020 --> 00:01:45.700
I have my project
on Google Cloud.

00:01:45.700 --> 00:01:48.405
I have my virtual
network in my project.

00:01:48.405 --> 00:01:50.610
I have my Kubernetes
engine cluster

00:01:50.610 --> 00:01:52.980
running in my virtual network.

00:01:52.980 --> 00:01:55.990
I have my workloads
running in the cluster.

00:01:55.990 --> 00:02:01.520
Now, how do I expose my
services to the internet?

00:02:01.520 --> 00:02:05.730
The answer is the Kubernetes
service abstraction.

00:02:05.730 --> 00:02:08.210
So the Kubernetes service
abstractions provide

00:02:08.210 --> 00:02:10.280
two major functionalities.

00:02:10.280 --> 00:02:15.410
First, it selects a group of
backend using label selectors.

00:02:15.410 --> 00:02:18.860
Second, it provides
static access point

00:02:18.860 --> 00:02:21.200
to these backend pods.

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:26.240
On the left, there's a
sample service specification.

00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:28.430
Its name is my service.

00:02:28.430 --> 00:02:33.590
It selects pods with
label key equals to app

00:02:33.590 --> 00:02:36.140
and value equals to MyApp.

00:02:36.140 --> 00:02:41.720
It exposes port 80, and
its type is ClusterIP.

00:02:41.720 --> 00:02:44.000
For external traffic
in particular,

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:47.900
there's node port type service,
and there is load balancer type

00:02:47.900 --> 00:02:50.150
service.

00:02:50.150 --> 00:02:52.790
Logically, the
diagram in the middle

00:02:52.790 --> 00:02:55.310
represents how
the traffic flows.

00:02:55.310 --> 00:02:59.270
The traffic first arrives
at the service abstraction,

00:02:59.270 --> 00:03:02.070
represented by the red icon.

00:03:02.070 --> 00:03:03.950
And then the service
will deliver the traffic

00:03:03.950 --> 00:03:07.910
to one of the backend pod
represented by the blue icons.

00:03:07.910 --> 00:03:11.870
On top of service, there's the
Ingress resource in Kubernetes.

00:03:11.870 --> 00:03:16.690
It represents level
seven load balancer.

00:03:16.690 --> 00:03:20.570
A node port type service
allocates a static port

00:03:20.570 --> 00:03:23.960
across all cluster nodes.

00:03:23.960 --> 00:03:29.570
Traffic flowing to the node
port on any VM or any node

00:03:29.570 --> 00:03:33.530
will be forwarded to
one of the backend pod.

00:03:33.530 --> 00:03:36.270
This model is very
easy to understand.

00:03:36.270 --> 00:03:38.400
There's no external dependency.

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:42.320
User just need to run the
standard open source kube-proxy

00:03:42.320 --> 00:03:44.630
to have this feature.

00:03:44.630 --> 00:03:46.640
The downsides are obvious.

00:03:46.640 --> 00:03:50.180
There are only a limited number
of node port within a cluster.

00:03:50.180 --> 00:03:54.350
And the clients need to
adjust to node IP changes.

00:03:54.350 --> 00:03:58.430
And node port only provides
basic load balancing algorithm,

00:03:58.430 --> 00:04:02.120
basically spreading
the traffic randomly.

00:04:02.120 --> 00:04:07.630
All this results into
suboptimal load balancing.

00:04:07.630 --> 00:04:12.110
In Kubernetes, there's a cloud
provider abstraction layer.

00:04:12.110 --> 00:04:16.540
This layer makes it easy
to integrate Kubernetes API

00:04:16.540 --> 00:04:18.940
with different infrastructure.

00:04:18.940 --> 00:04:24.220
This makes Kubernetes' API
portable across platforms.

00:04:24.220 --> 00:04:27.490
When user creates a load
balancer type service,

00:04:27.490 --> 00:04:30.790
a real load balancer
will be provisioned.

00:04:30.790 --> 00:04:34.540
It usually comes with
an external virtual IP.

00:04:34.540 --> 00:04:38.760
Traffic first flow
to the virtual IP,

00:04:38.760 --> 00:04:41.530
it arrives at the load
balancer, and the load balancer

00:04:41.530 --> 00:04:45.380
will deliver the packet
to Kubernetes service.

00:04:45.380 --> 00:04:48.370
In most integrations,
the load balancer

00:04:48.370 --> 00:04:51.610
would just deliver the packet
to a node port type service.

00:04:51.610 --> 00:04:54.280
And the service will
further deliver the packet

00:04:54.280 --> 00:04:57.460
to the backend pods.

00:04:57.460 --> 00:05:01.060
Kubernetes services are
level four constructs.

00:05:01.060 --> 00:05:04.120
In order to support level
seven functionalities,

00:05:04.120 --> 00:05:06.430
Ingress was proposed.

00:05:06.430 --> 00:05:10.240
Ingress supports popular
level seven functionalities

00:05:10.240 --> 00:05:14.680
like TLS termination,
URL path mapping.

00:05:14.680 --> 00:05:17.230
There are many Ingress
implementations.

00:05:17.230 --> 00:05:20.260
On Kubernetes'
engine, it utilize

00:05:20.260 --> 00:05:25.930
Google Cloud load balancer
to implement the Ingress API.

00:05:25.930 --> 00:05:28.600
Conceptually,
Ingress traffic first

00:05:28.600 --> 00:05:30.700
arrives at the Ingress layer.

00:05:30.700 --> 00:05:33.160
That can be a Google
Cloud load balancer.

00:05:33.160 --> 00:05:35.950
Then the level seven
functionalities

00:05:35.950 --> 00:05:40.420
kicks in, TLS termination
and URL path mapping.

00:05:40.420 --> 00:05:42.670
And the load balancer
will deliver the traffic

00:05:42.670 --> 00:05:44.920
to the corresponding service.

00:05:44.920 --> 00:05:49.450
And the service will take
it to the backend pods.

00:05:49.450 --> 00:05:52.510
We have briefly walk
through the Kubernetes API

00:05:52.510 --> 00:05:55.090
constructs for load balancing.

00:05:55.090 --> 00:05:57.990
Although the API
seems straightforward,

00:05:57.990 --> 00:06:02.440
but the underneath data path
can be very, very complex.

00:06:02.440 --> 00:06:05.620
To understand what is container
native load balancing,

00:06:05.620 --> 00:06:10.220
we need to take a closer
look at the data path.

00:06:10.220 --> 00:06:15.280
Here is a typical Ingress
setup on Kubernetes engine.

00:06:15.280 --> 00:06:20.250
All other cloud providers
have some similar setups.

00:06:20.250 --> 00:06:24.030
When user creates an
Ingress, Kubernetes Engine

00:06:24.030 --> 00:06:27.084
will create a Google
Cloud load balancer.

00:06:27.084 --> 00:06:28.500
And the Google
Cloud load balancer

00:06:28.500 --> 00:06:30.550
will point to an instance group.

00:06:30.550 --> 00:06:34.380
The instance group
contains all cluster nodes.

00:06:34.380 --> 00:06:37.920
And kube-proxy is responsible
for forwarding traffic

00:06:37.920 --> 00:06:40.380
to the actual backend pop.

00:06:40.380 --> 00:06:43.540
The name of kube-proxy
is quite misleading.

00:06:43.540 --> 00:06:45.310
It's not a real proxy.

00:06:45.310 --> 00:06:50.640
It programs iptables and ask
the Linux kernel to do its job.

00:06:53.580 --> 00:06:58.440
So this setup results in
suboptimal load balancing.

00:06:58.440 --> 00:07:01.980
As you can see in the
animation, the blue dot

00:07:01.980 --> 00:07:05.000
represents the incoming traffic.

00:07:05.000 --> 00:07:08.160
Load balancer sends packet
to the virtual machine,

00:07:08.160 --> 00:07:11.700
and kube-proxy programs
iptables to intercept traffic

00:07:11.700 --> 00:07:13.530
from the load balancer.

00:07:13.530 --> 00:07:17.260
Then the iptables
magic took place.

00:07:17.260 --> 00:07:21.210
iptables randomly select
one of the backend pod,

00:07:21.210 --> 00:07:25.110
perform network address
translation, port translation,

00:07:25.110 --> 00:07:29.940
and finally send the packet
to the destination pod.

00:07:29.940 --> 00:07:33.960
Because the backend pod
selection is randomized,

00:07:33.960 --> 00:07:40.140
the iptables can select a pod
on a different node that will

00:07:40.140 --> 00:07:44.810
result in a second network hop,

00:07:44.810 --> 00:07:50.580
For the response packet, it will
reverse the incoming data path.

00:07:50.580 --> 00:07:53.780
So the pod will send
out a response packet.

00:07:53.780 --> 00:07:58.390
The packet will first arrived
in the forwarding node.

00:07:58.390 --> 00:08:02.270
On the forwarding,
iptables and contract table

00:08:02.270 --> 00:08:06.500
will undo all the network
address translation

00:08:06.500 --> 00:08:08.090
and port translation.

00:08:08.090 --> 00:08:13.340
And then the packet will return
back to the load balancer.

00:08:13.340 --> 00:08:17.110
So with all these, it's
very, very complex.

00:08:17.110 --> 00:08:20.120
There's a lot going on
just to deliver packet

00:08:20.120 --> 00:08:23.320
to the container.

00:08:23.320 --> 00:08:27.810
Now, we are proud to announce
container native load balancing

00:08:27.810 --> 00:08:32.010
using network endpoint group
feature on Google Cloud.

00:08:32.010 --> 00:08:34.500
Network endpoint
group feature allows

00:08:34.500 --> 00:08:39.330
Google Cloud load balancer to
target containers directly.

00:08:39.330 --> 00:08:43.289
Containers become
just another endpoint.

00:08:43.289 --> 00:08:47.430
As you can see in the animation,
there's no IP table magic.

00:08:47.430 --> 00:08:50.940
There's no proxy involved, no
need for the complex network

00:08:50.940 --> 00:08:52.380
address translation.

00:08:52.380 --> 00:08:56.660
And of course,
there's no second hop.

00:08:56.660 --> 00:09:00.090
Every connection is
established directly

00:09:00.090 --> 00:09:05.000
between the load balancer
and the backend pod.

00:09:05.000 --> 00:09:08.870
So let us have a
side-by-side comparison.

00:09:08.870 --> 00:09:11.590
The data path becomes
much, much cleaner.

00:09:16.100 --> 00:09:18.230
So what difference does it make?

00:09:18.230 --> 00:09:21.250
Network endpoint groups
makes load balancing

00:09:21.250 --> 00:09:25.690
for containers as
efficient and as easy

00:09:25.690 --> 00:09:30.730
as load balancing
for virtual machines.

00:09:30.730 --> 00:09:32.740
So let's summarize.

00:09:32.740 --> 00:09:36.600
Network endpoint groups makes
container a first class citizen

00:09:36.600 --> 00:09:38.920
on Google Cloud load balancer.

00:09:38.920 --> 00:09:44.020
It opens the door for native
container support for many load

00:09:44.020 --> 00:09:47.970
balancer features such as
traffic shaping, advanced load

00:09:47.970 --> 00:09:50.870
balancing algorithms,
and many more.

00:09:50.870 --> 00:09:54.250
Take traffic shaping
as an example.

00:09:54.250 --> 00:09:56.850
Without network endpoint
group, load balancers

00:09:56.850 --> 00:10:01.560
can only do traffic accounting
per virtual machines.

00:10:01.560 --> 00:10:03.360
With the secondary
load balancing

00:10:03.360 --> 00:10:07.740
happen on the virtual machine,
load balancer loses track.

00:10:07.740 --> 00:10:10.950
Switching traffic
between VMs doesn't

00:10:10.950 --> 00:10:16.290
result in switching traffic
among the actual backend pod.

00:10:16.290 --> 00:10:18.990
With network endpoint
group, load balancer

00:10:18.990 --> 00:10:22.560
gains the visibility
into each traffic flowing

00:10:22.560 --> 00:10:26.710
to every containers accurately.

00:10:26.710 --> 00:10:30.450
Then traffic shaping is
achievable at the container

00:10:30.450 --> 00:10:31.830
level.

00:10:31.830 --> 00:10:35.280
Secondly, data
path is optimized.

00:10:35.280 --> 00:10:38.670
There's no longer any proxy
between the load balancer

00:10:38.670 --> 00:10:40.620
and the backend pod.

00:10:40.620 --> 00:10:44.910
That eliminates the
second network hop.

00:10:44.910 --> 00:10:48.900
This reduces the latency
and removes the overhead

00:10:48.900 --> 00:10:54.000
for the complex network
address translation and results

00:10:54.000 --> 00:10:58.270
as a predictable
network performance.

00:10:58.270 --> 00:11:02.700
Finally, tools that integrate it
with Google Cloud load balancer

00:11:02.700 --> 00:11:08.490
gets visibility at the
container level automatically.

00:11:08.490 --> 00:11:14.310
Load balancer health check can
retrieve accurate health status

00:11:14.310 --> 00:11:16.140
per container.

00:11:16.140 --> 00:11:19.210
Without network endpoint
groups, load balancer

00:11:19.210 --> 00:11:21.630
sends the health check
to the virtual machines,

00:11:21.630 --> 00:11:24.570
and virtual machines force
the health check request

00:11:24.570 --> 00:11:25.980
all over the place.

00:11:25.980 --> 00:11:31.740
That makes the health
check result unreliable.

00:11:31.740 --> 00:11:37.100
With all that, I would like to
introduce my colleague, Manjot.

00:11:37.100 --> 00:11:41.060
[APPLAUSE]

00:11:43.499 --> 00:11:45.290
MANJOT PAHWA: Thank
you, Minhan, for making

00:11:45.290 --> 00:11:46.850
load balancing boring again.

00:11:46.850 --> 00:11:49.610
[LAUGHTER]

00:11:49.610 --> 00:11:54.050
Minhan just gave us an overview
of how our load balancing is

00:11:54.050 --> 00:11:56.120
becoming more container native.

00:11:56.120 --> 00:11:59.960
I will now touch upon how
our network services are also

00:11:59.960 --> 00:12:02.240
becoming more container native.

00:12:02.240 --> 00:12:04.520
Google Cloud Platform
Network Services

00:12:04.520 --> 00:12:06.860
have the mission
to deliver services

00:12:06.860 --> 00:12:11.030
that are smart, secure, and
modern on the Google Cloud

00:12:11.030 --> 00:12:13.520
platform and other clouds.

00:12:13.520 --> 00:12:16.670
We bring these strengths
to your Kubernetes Engine

00:12:16.670 --> 00:12:20.090
deployments via Ingress
and a host of other feature

00:12:20.090 --> 00:12:22.910
improvements.

00:12:22.910 --> 00:12:25.340
We all know that
Ingress is your one stop

00:12:25.340 --> 00:12:28.490
shop for configuring
all your L7 needs,

00:12:28.490 --> 00:12:33.590
including your global HTTPS
Google Cloud load balancer.

00:12:33.590 --> 00:12:37.070
Up until now, you could
set your host path

00:12:37.070 --> 00:12:39.500
routing rules via
Ingress to be set up

00:12:39.500 --> 00:12:43.840
at the HTTPS Google
Cloud load balancer using

00:12:43.840 --> 00:12:47.120
Kubernetes' native Ingress API.

00:12:47.120 --> 00:12:50.360
We have been working
at Kubernetes Engine

00:12:50.360 --> 00:12:53.480
to bring Kubernetes
Engine networking at par

00:12:53.480 --> 00:12:56.930
with Compute Engine and
establishing containers

00:12:56.930 --> 00:12:59.480
as first class endpoints.

00:12:59.480 --> 00:13:03.350
With these changes, you will
be able to use the Kubernetes

00:13:03.350 --> 00:13:09.470
Ingress API to configure
L7 security, authorization

00:13:09.470 --> 00:13:13.910
for your employees, and
use a globally distributed

00:13:13.910 --> 00:13:16.990
caching infrastructure.

00:13:16.990 --> 00:13:20.960
Configure security policies
for defense at scale

00:13:20.960 --> 00:13:24.110
against distributed
denial of service attacks

00:13:24.110 --> 00:13:28.010
with cloud armor using
Google's global infrastructure

00:13:28.010 --> 00:13:30.050
and security systems.

00:13:30.050 --> 00:13:34.040
Use cloud armor in your
Kubernetes Engine deployments

00:13:34.040 --> 00:13:37.800
to protect against malicious
attacks at the edge,

00:13:37.800 --> 00:13:40.860
and restrict access
to the load balancer.

00:13:40.860 --> 00:13:43.970
I would recommend going to
the session on Cloud Armor

00:13:43.970 --> 00:13:46.090
by Prajakta.

00:13:46.090 --> 00:13:49.780
Control access to your
internal applications

00:13:49.780 --> 00:13:54.640
at application level granularity
using identity-based access

00:13:54.640 --> 00:13:58.360
control with
identity-aware proxy.

00:13:58.360 --> 00:14:01.370
Cloud IAP, or
identity-aware proxy,

00:14:01.370 --> 00:14:04.690
lets you establish a
central authorization

00:14:04.690 --> 00:14:09.550
for all your applications
accessed by HTTPS so that you

00:14:09.550 --> 00:14:13.240
can use an application-level
access control instead

00:14:13.240 --> 00:14:16.570
of the older network-level
firewalls, which gives you

00:14:16.570 --> 00:14:22.390
an all-or-nothing
access-level granularity.

00:14:22.390 --> 00:14:25.810
Third, leverage a
globally distributed

00:14:25.810 --> 00:14:28.600
caching infrastructure
for all your low latency

00:14:28.600 --> 00:14:32.140
