00:14:47.420 --> 00:14:49.750
Together, with the
above capabilities,

00:14:49.750 --> 00:14:53.230
you can build applications
on Kubernetes engine

00:14:53.230 --> 00:14:56.350
that are smart,
secure, and modern.

00:14:56.350 --> 00:14:58.810
We will demonstrate
the integration

00:14:58.810 --> 00:15:01.750
of these new
capabilities and how

00:15:01.750 --> 00:15:05.050
Ingress on Google
Cloud Platform becomes

00:15:05.050 --> 00:15:09.920
the single pane of configuration
for all your L7 needs.

00:15:09.920 --> 00:15:12.880
Let's jump into a little more
detail on how we can do that.

00:15:21.900 --> 00:15:26.690
So we introduced a new concept
called the BackendConfig.

00:15:26.690 --> 00:15:31.940
But before we do
that, let's jump

00:15:31.940 --> 00:15:34.160
into a little more history.

00:15:34.160 --> 00:15:37.580
As you might already know,
Kubernetes Ingress API

00:15:37.580 --> 00:15:40.160
is the open source
and portable way

00:15:40.160 --> 00:15:43.340
to express your
L7 configuration.

00:15:43.340 --> 00:15:45.170
Since it was meant
to be portable,

00:15:45.170 --> 00:15:48.920
the only way to have
provider-specific information

00:15:48.920 --> 00:15:51.950
was through Ingress Annotations.

00:15:51.950 --> 00:15:55.490
This made the integration of
new features either dependent

00:15:55.490 --> 00:15:57.650
on the approval
of the community,

00:15:57.650 --> 00:16:00.770
affecting the speed of
overall development,

00:16:00.770 --> 00:16:03.740
or an overpopulation
of annotations

00:16:03.740 --> 00:16:05.960
on your Ingress definition.

00:16:05.960 --> 00:16:07.820
Maintaining this
delicate balance

00:16:07.820 --> 00:16:10.190
between provider-specific
information

00:16:10.190 --> 00:16:15.590
and an Ingress portability is
not an easy question to answer.

00:16:15.590 --> 00:16:21.780
So BackendConfig aims to
address exactly that problem.

00:16:21.780 --> 00:16:26.250
We introduce BackendConfig
to specify configuration

00:16:26.250 --> 00:16:28.780
on a per service basis.

00:16:28.780 --> 00:16:34.650
Use BackendConfig for your
Google Cloud Platform network

00:16:34.650 --> 00:16:39.190
services like Cloud Armor,
identity-aware proxy, and cloud

00:16:39.190 --> 00:16:41.240
CDN to begin with.

00:16:41.240 --> 00:16:45.290
BackendConfig is a new
Kubernetes custom resource

00:16:45.290 --> 00:16:47.760
that is a Kubernetes
native concept

00:16:47.760 --> 00:16:51.240
and is specific to
Google Cloud Platform.

00:16:51.240 --> 00:16:53.880
BackendConfig lets you
specify configuration

00:16:53.880 --> 00:16:57.180
for all your services
without the need of polluting

00:16:57.180 --> 00:17:00.180
your Ingress definition
with too many annotations

00:17:00.180 --> 00:17:02.430
as was the case in
the past, and which

00:17:02.430 --> 00:17:05.430
makes your ingress
definition non-portable.

00:17:05.430 --> 00:17:09.960
This simplifies your experience
with the Ingress API.

00:17:09.960 --> 00:17:13.950
There's native integration with
all the GCP network services,

00:17:13.950 --> 00:17:15.900
giving you a host of benefits.

00:17:15.900 --> 00:17:20.490
And service configuration
becomes a first-class concept.

00:17:20.490 --> 00:17:24.240
You have the flexibility
to specify configuration

00:17:24.240 --> 00:17:29.010
pertaining to a single service
rather than an all-or-nothing

00:17:29.010 --> 00:17:31.740
for your whole deployment.

00:17:31.740 --> 00:17:34.500
You can hook up your
service definition

00:17:34.500 --> 00:17:40.080
using BackendConfig to use
all these network services.

00:17:40.080 --> 00:17:42.760
Now, let's move on to
some of the new features

00:17:42.760 --> 00:17:45.690
that we have for Ingress on
the Google Cloud Platform.

00:17:45.690 --> 00:17:48.130
Minhan already mentioned
that Ingress can

00:17:48.130 --> 00:17:50.440
be used for TLS termination.

00:17:50.440 --> 00:17:55.150
Today, we announce the
availability of multiple TLS--

00:17:55.150 --> 00:18:00.070
multiple SSL certificate support
through your Ingress definition

00:18:00.070 --> 00:18:03.490
instead of the older way of
bundling multiple domains

00:18:03.490 --> 00:18:06.400
into a single certificate
or having multiple Ingress

00:18:06.400 --> 00:18:10.330
definitions that adds overhead.

00:18:10.330 --> 00:18:13.660
We also try to ensure
at Kubernetes Engine

00:18:13.660 --> 00:18:17.140
that your Ingress remains
competitive in the integration

00:18:17.140 --> 00:18:21.340
of new protocols
and new technology.

00:18:21.340 --> 00:18:25.060
Ingress now supports
HTTP/2 protocol,

00:18:25.060 --> 00:18:28.720
which is designed to
improve performance

00:18:28.720 --> 00:18:32.530
over its predecessor, which
is the HTTP/1 protocol.

00:18:32.530 --> 00:18:36.130
Support for HTTP/2
enables you to use

00:18:36.130 --> 00:18:41.320
gRPC, which is an open source
framework for remote procedure

00:18:41.320 --> 00:18:44.920
calls based on the
HTTP/2 standard.

00:18:44.920 --> 00:18:49.480
gRPC enables you
creation of performant,

00:18:49.480 --> 00:18:54.640
scalable APIs and micro services
in many programming languages

00:18:54.640 --> 00:18:57.190
and platforms.

00:18:57.190 --> 00:19:01.960
To summarize what we
have mentioned until now,

00:19:01.960 --> 00:19:04.270
we bring you a host
of new capabilities

00:19:04.270 --> 00:19:07.720
and features to configure
at L7 via Ingress

00:19:07.720 --> 00:19:09.820
on the Google Cloud Platform--

00:19:09.820 --> 00:19:12.310
the concept of
BackendConfig, which

00:19:12.310 --> 00:19:15.250
lets you set configuration
on a per service

00:19:15.250 --> 00:19:18.190
basis without the need
of polluting your Ingress

00:19:18.190 --> 00:19:20.590
definition with too
many annotations

00:19:20.590 --> 00:19:24.040
and gives you flexibility to
specify things on a per service

00:19:24.040 --> 00:19:26.740
basis rather than for
your whole deployment;

00:19:26.740 --> 00:19:30.100
native integration with Google
Cloud Platform's network

00:19:30.100 --> 00:19:35.080
services, which lets you get
a host of benefits, including

00:19:35.080 --> 00:19:37.810
security at L7
using Cloud Armor,

00:19:37.810 --> 00:19:41.050
identity-based access
control for your applications

00:19:41.050 --> 00:19:45.820
via identity-aware proxy, and
a globally distributed caching

00:19:45.820 --> 00:19:49.760
infrastructure using cloud CDN;
Ingress on the Google Cloud

00:19:49.760 --> 00:19:53.320
Platform also lets you
use multiple SSL certs

00:19:53.320 --> 00:19:57.700
for your deployment and use
new protocols such as HTTP/2

00:19:57.700 --> 00:19:59.670
and gRPC.

00:19:59.670 --> 00:20:02.190
With that, I'll
hand over to Bowei,

00:20:02.190 --> 00:20:05.340
who is the Kubernetes
Engine TL, to show

00:20:05.340 --> 00:20:08.050
you a host of new features.

00:20:08.050 --> 00:20:11.890
[APPLAUSE]

00:20:13.340 --> 00:20:14.650
BOWEI DU: OK, hello.

00:20:14.650 --> 00:20:16.840
Thanks, Manjot.

00:20:16.840 --> 00:20:20.530
So Kubernetes Engine makes it
easy to create Google Cloud

00:20:20.530 --> 00:20:21.220
load balancers.

00:20:21.220 --> 00:20:22.720
And it's full of
features as you've

00:20:22.720 --> 00:20:25.300
seen in the previous slides.

00:20:25.300 --> 00:20:26.920
But we've heard
lots of questions

00:20:26.920 --> 00:20:30.130
about how to extend your
applications using Ingress

00:20:30.130 --> 00:20:32.210
across multiple
clusters, whether it's

00:20:32.210 --> 00:20:34.990
for fault tolerance or
to optimize latency.

00:20:34.990 --> 00:20:37.750
And what I'm going to show you
is a sneak peek of something

00:20:37.750 --> 00:20:40.450
that we're currently working on.

00:20:40.450 --> 00:20:42.370
So suppose you create an app.

00:20:42.370 --> 00:20:45.040
You deploy it on Kubernetes
Engine in North America,

00:20:45.040 --> 00:20:47.120
and you have users
coming to your cluster.

00:20:47.120 --> 00:20:48.850
Now, your app gets very popular.

00:20:48.850 --> 00:20:51.200
Actually, popular
all over the world.

00:20:51.200 --> 00:20:55.590
Now you have users coming
from London, Singapore,

00:20:55.590 --> 00:20:57.180
all over from Europe, Asia.

00:20:57.180 --> 00:20:59.580
But your application
is still being served

00:20:59.580 --> 00:21:01.560
from a North American cluster.

00:21:01.560 --> 00:21:03.300
Note that the Google
Cloud load balancer

00:21:03.300 --> 00:21:05.910
will terminate your
connections at the Google edge.

00:21:05.910 --> 00:21:09.580
You'll see there's a Google
edge in North America, Europe,

00:21:09.580 --> 00:21:11.820
and Singapore, which
gives your users

00:21:11.820 --> 00:21:14.820
the benefits of low latency
protocol termination.

00:21:14.820 --> 00:21:17.190
However, the application
will be still served

00:21:17.190 --> 00:21:19.110
from your North
America location,

00:21:19.110 --> 00:21:21.750
so the end-to-end application
latency may be higher

00:21:21.750 --> 00:21:24.450
for some users in the world.

00:21:24.450 --> 00:21:30.540
Now, today you can geolocalize
your application by using DNS.

00:21:30.540 --> 00:21:34.810
With this setup, you can create
an Ingress for each cluster

00:21:34.810 --> 00:21:37.060
you're serving in
the region that you

00:21:37.060 --> 00:21:39.820
want it to serve and then
have a different VIP for each

00:21:39.820 --> 00:21:43.390
and put that virtual
IP in your DNS record.

00:21:43.390 --> 00:21:45.970
While this gives you
geolocalization, as you know,

00:21:45.970 --> 00:21:49.390
DNS has suboptimal load
balancing and failure

00:21:49.390 --> 00:21:50.865
over characteristics.

00:21:50.865 --> 00:21:52.240
In addition, you
need to maintain

00:21:52.240 --> 00:21:54.640
multiple copies of
the Ingress resource

00:21:54.640 --> 00:21:56.470
and synchronize them
among your clusters.

00:21:56.470 --> 00:21:59.290
Also, you need to
set up geo-DNS.

00:21:59.290 --> 00:22:04.570
So we are excited to
announce an upcoming feature

00:22:04.570 --> 00:22:07.480
in Kubernetes Engine,
which is multi-cluster and

00:22:07.480 --> 00:22:09.250
multi-regional Ingress support.

00:22:09.250 --> 00:22:12.820
With this feature, you'll
be able to write one Ingress

00:22:12.820 --> 00:22:16.015
definition and use Google
Cloud load balancer

00:22:16.015 --> 00:22:19.510
to distribute traffic among the
clusters from a single anycast

00:22:19.510 --> 00:22:20.620
IP.

00:22:20.620 --> 00:22:24.400
Now, as your user base
expands, say, worldwide,

00:22:24.400 --> 00:22:28.960
you will be able to add
clusters in each region

00:22:28.960 --> 00:22:30.880
where you will have
users, following

00:22:30.880 --> 00:22:34.000
your user population as needed.

00:22:34.000 --> 00:22:35.740
As you see from the
diagram, there'll

00:22:35.740 --> 00:22:39.510
be a single virtual IP that will
be programmed into your DNS.

00:22:39.510 --> 00:22:41.290
And traffic from
each of your clusters

00:22:41.290 --> 00:22:43.090
will be routed to
the closest one.

00:22:43.090 --> 00:22:46.602
In fact, if capacity is exceeded
or if a cluster goes down,

00:22:46.602 --> 00:22:48.310
your user will be
routed to the next best

00:22:48.310 --> 00:22:53.300
destination automatically
as failure is detected.

00:22:53.300 --> 00:22:55.960
Now, actually, let's
take a hands-on look

00:22:55.960 --> 00:23:00.680
at some of the details
about how this will work.

00:23:00.680 --> 00:23:04.410
So, give me one second.

00:23:04.410 --> 00:23:07.830
OK, so here we have
a dashboard that

00:23:07.830 --> 00:23:10.980
is wired up to a live
instance of the multi-cluster,

00:23:10.980 --> 00:23:12.780
multi-regional Ingress.

00:23:12.780 --> 00:23:16.410
And as you see, following
the workflow from before,

00:23:16.410 --> 00:23:18.930
we have a North
American cluster.

00:23:18.930 --> 00:23:21.330
And most of our users
are from North America,

00:23:21.330 --> 00:23:24.510
so they experience
acceptable latency.

00:23:24.510 --> 00:23:27.660
We have a few users from
Asia, but not too many.

00:23:27.660 --> 00:23:30.690
Now, as we increase the
traffic, because our app got

00:23:30.690 --> 00:23:33.990
super popular, we got a
lot of users from Asia.

00:23:33.990 --> 00:23:36.690
But you'll notice that they're
still hitting the North

00:23:36.690 --> 00:23:38.850
American cluster.

00:23:38.850 --> 00:23:42.090
With multi-cluster and
multi-regional Ingress,

00:23:42.090 --> 00:23:45.300
we can actually add a cluster.

00:23:45.300 --> 00:23:47.690
You see that we added
a cluster in Asia,

00:23:47.690 --> 00:23:50.410
but we haven't
updated the routing.

00:23:50.410 --> 00:23:53.730
Now, finally, we will
update the routing

00:23:53.730 --> 00:23:56.820
so that this cluster is
included in the load balancer.

00:23:56.820 --> 00:23:59.160
And as you see,
the users in Asia

00:23:59.160 --> 00:24:01.140
will go to the Asia cluster.

00:24:01.140 --> 00:24:03.930
And the users in North America
will go to the North America

00:24:03.930 --> 00:24:05.740
cluster.

00:24:05.740 --> 00:24:09.720
Now, let's poke a little bit
in terms of what this actually

00:24:09.720 --> 00:24:10.660
looks like.

00:24:10.660 --> 00:24:14.880
So here is the Ingress
definition for this setup.

00:24:14.880 --> 00:24:17.640
You notice that it is just
the vanilla Kubernetes Ingress

00:24:17.640 --> 00:24:18.900
definition.

00:24:18.900 --> 00:24:21.150
And in fact, when
you go and look

00:24:21.150 --> 00:24:24.900
at your network service
in Google Cloud UI,

00:24:24.900 --> 00:24:27.990
you'll see that it sets
up a single virtual IP.

00:24:27.990 --> 00:24:34.440
And the difference is you
have two backend services, one

00:24:34.440 --> 00:24:37.860
of them in Asia and one
of them in North America.

00:24:37.860 --> 00:24:42.180
Finally, let's see what
happens for your users, right?

00:24:42.180 --> 00:24:44.950
Your users, we have
clients in Asia,

00:24:44.950 --> 00:24:47.280
and we have clients
in North America.

00:24:47.280 --> 00:24:51.660
So here's an access log
that's been aggregated

00:24:51.660 --> 00:24:54.150
across the clusters,
showing where

00:24:54.150 --> 00:24:57.960
the user request was coming from
and which cluster is served.

00:24:57.960 --> 00:25:01.860
And as you can see, requests
coming from North America

00:25:01.860 --> 00:25:04.230
are served by the
North American server,

00:25:04.230 --> 00:25:06.510
and requests coming
from Asia are being

00:25:06.510 --> 00:25:10.890
served by the Asia cluster.

00:25:10.890 --> 00:25:12.877
Now, switching back
to the presentation.

00:25:16.010 --> 00:25:19.340
OK, so what are the benefits
of multi-cluster and

00:25:19.340 --> 00:25:20.940
multi-regional support?

00:25:20.940 --> 00:25:23.100
So notice, you have a
single configuration

00:25:23.100 --> 00:25:24.840
for your global applications.

00:25:24.840 --> 00:25:28.170
You will use a single
standard Ingress definition

00:25:28.170 --> 00:25:30.990
that you already use to
define your HTTP applications.

00:25:30.990 --> 00:25:33.420
This definition will
be used to configure

00:25:33.420 --> 00:25:36.810
a global multi-cluster
Google Cloud load balancer,

00:25:36.810 --> 00:25:41.280
giving you one place for
configuration and status.

00:25:41.280 --> 00:25:43.050
The multi-cluster,
multi-regional Ingress

00:25:43.050 --> 00:25:45.660
will use network endpoint
groups to address your cluster

00:25:45.660 --> 00:25:46.710
endpoints.

00:25:46.710 --> 00:25:51.210
And as you saw earlier, this has
all the advantages of container

00:25:51.210 --> 00:25:52.580
native load balancing.

00:25:52.580 --> 00:25:54.900
You have accurate health
checks, better load balancing

00:25:54.900 --> 00:25:57.730
algorithms, and optimal
network performance.

00:25:57.730 --> 00:25:59.760
And finally, the most
straightforward point, you

00:25:59.760 --> 00:26:02.970
have geographical load
balancing and high availability.

00:26:02.970 --> 00:26:05.370
And using this feature will
make your Kubernetes apps

00:26:05.370 --> 00:26:10.070
and services
globally distributed.

00:26:10.070 --> 00:26:12.590
So as you saw in
this talk, there's

00:26:12.590 --> 00:26:15.290
a little bit of history
about where container load

00:26:15.290 --> 00:26:20.000
balancing was and
some indications

00:26:20.000 --> 00:26:22.580
of what we think the
final form should be.

00:26:22.580 --> 00:26:25.710
And the final form should
be container native load

00:26:25.710 --> 00:26:26.810
balancing.

00:26:26.810 --> 00:26:28.820
What is container
native load balancing?

00:26:28.820 --> 00:26:31.070
First and foremost, it
means that containers

00:26:31.070 --> 00:26:32.960
have optimal network transport.

00:26:32.960 --> 00:26:35.090
Load balancers are
container aware.

00:26:35.090 --> 00:26:37.580
There should be no difference
whether your workload

00:26:37.580 --> 00:26:39.110
uses containers or VMs.

00:26:39.110 --> 00:26:40.760
It should just work.

00:26:40.760 --> 00:26:43.760
Second, your container workloads
can use Google Cloud load

00:26:43.760 --> 00:26:44.960
balancer feature set.

00:26:44.960 --> 00:26:48.830
For example, security, caching,
authentication in a Kubernetes

00:26:48.830 --> 00:26:52.710
native way, using Kubernetes
resources and configuration.

00:26:52.710 --> 00:26:54.890
Finally, coming soon,
Kubernetes engine

00:26:54.890 --> 00:26:57.050
will support extending
load balancing

00:26:57.050 --> 00:26:58.700
across multiple
clusters in regions,

00:26:58.700 --> 00:27:03.020
giving your applications global
reach and high availability.

00:27:03.020 --> 00:27:05.810
We want to make it so that
any choice of technology,

00:27:05.810 --> 00:27:09.740
whether it's containers or VMs,
runs the best on Google Cloud.

00:27:09.740 --> 00:27:11.300
And container native
load balancing

00:27:11.300 --> 00:27:13.376
is a piece of that puzzle.

00:27:13.376 --> 00:27:14.372
Thank you.

00:27:14.372 --> 00:27:17.360
[APPLAUSE]

00:27:18.854 --> 00:27:23.590
